 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "GLENDALOUGH. In Irish, Gleann Dá Loch, 'the valley of the two lakes'. St Kevin settled here in the 6th century to be alone. A thousand monks promptly arrived to be alone near him."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE ROUND TOWER. About 30 m tall, with a door three metres off the ground. A bell tower and a lookout and, if the Vikings were about, a very tall panic room. The ladder came up with the monks."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "THE LOWER LAKE. Glacier-carved, 15,000 years ago, and still waiting for someone to say thank you. The sika deer often come to drink at dusk, and go through your sandwiches at dawn."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "THE UPPER LAKE. St Kevin's bed is a cave in the cliffs above it. He is said to have slept on rock, which is how you know he was a saint, or an extremely committed camper."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "THE WICKLOW WAY. 130 km of trail across the mountains, Ireland's oldest waymarked walk. The yellow walker on the posts is not a ghost. It's a signpost."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "SALLY GAP, THIS WAY. A road over the top of the mountains, famous for its views and its fog. You will see one of them."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 17,
   "plaque": "wicklow_military_road",
   "title": "The Military Road",
   "text": "After the rebellion of 1798, rebels such as Michael Dwyer held out in the Wicklow hills for years. So the British army built the Military Road across the mountains, right through the Sally Gap, to reach them. Today it's walkers who use it, and sheep."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 46,
   "plaque": "wicklow_gold_rush",
   "title": "The Wicklow Gold Rush",
   "text": "In 1795 gold was found in a stream on Croghan Kinshelagh in south Wicklow, and thousands of people rushed to pan for it. One nugget weighed more than 600 grams. The army took the diggings over within weeks. The gold is mostly gone. The stream is still there."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "arts_synge",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "J. M. Synge",
   "text": "John Millington Synge, born in 1871, spent his summers in these mountains and wrote them into The Shadow of the Glen in 1903: a lonely cottage, a tramp and a wife who leaves. Yeats sent him to the Aran Islands to listen, and the speech he heard there and in Wicklow became The Playboy of the Western World in 1907, which caused a riot. He died in 1909, aged 37, with the essays In Wicklow and West Kerry still in his drawer. The glens are quieter than his dialogue, which is saying something."
  }
 ],
